{"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"planarLayout"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphDrawing\/documentation\/GraphDrawing\/Drawing","text":"Drawing","preciseIdentifier":"s:12GraphDrawing0B0V"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Vertex","text":"Vertex","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa"},{"kind":"text","text":">?"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"]}]},{"content":[{"anchor":"return-value","text":"Return Value","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The drawing, in ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order, or nil when the graph has no planar embedding.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"},{"content":[{"text":"Discussion","type":"heading","anchor":"discussion","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The drawing comes from ","type":"text"},{"code":"planarEmbedding()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" by de Fraysseix, Pach and Pollack’s shift method","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in Chrobak and Payne’s linear-time form. The embedding of the underlying simple graph","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(self-loops and parallel copies drawn as one segment) is first made connected, by an edge","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"from the first vertex of each component to the first vertex of the next, and every face","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"boundary a simple cycle; then every face but the largest (the outer face) is triangulated.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"The vertices are placed in a canonical ordering of that graph, each one above the contour","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"of those before it, the contour shifted to make room. The added edges are not drawn and not","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"reported. Every vertex has integer coordinates with 0 ≤ x ≤ 2n − 4 and 0 ≤ y ≤ n − 2, and no","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"two edges cross or overlap. Fewer than four vertices are placed at (0, 0), (2, 0) and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(1, 1) in ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order. The drawing is not rescaled (","type":"text"},{"code":"rescaled(scale:center:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" maps it","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"to a common frame). A directed graph is drawn through ","type":"text"},{"code":"undirectedView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"de Fraysseix, Pach and Pollack, “How to draw a planar graph on a grid”, Combinatorica","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"10(1), 1990.","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Chrobak and Payne, “A linear-time algorithm for drawing a planar graph on a grid”,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Information Processing Letters 54(4), 1995."}]}]}]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"O(n + m) for the embedding, then O(n) expected (hashing the triangulation’s vertex pairs).","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"As for ","type":"text"},{"code":"planarEmbedding()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}],"type":"aside","style":"note","name":"Precondition"}],"kind":"content"}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"abstract":[{"text":"A crossing-free straight-line drawing on an integer grid, or nil when the graph is not","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"tex